  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nNo significant change to our weather is expected through the\nweekend. One subtle difference today is that the surface high\npressure over the Ohio Valley that was responsible for our very\nlight southwesterly gradient Wednesday has moved east and has \nbeen replaced with a new high over the upper Great Lakes. This\nwill result in a very light east or northeasterly gradient\ntoday, which should facilitate a slightly more formidable lake\nbreeze that will spread farther inland this afternoon. This\nshould result in temps falling a few degrees into the 60s this\nafternoon near the lake, however the more potentially meaningful\nimpact from the lake breeze will be the advection of higher\ndewpoint marine layer inland into northeast Illinois.\n\nGiven the ideal radiational cooling set up tonight, the higher \ndewpoints could set the stage for areas of dense fog to develop\nover northeast Illinois. The area most susceptible appears to \nbe the central and northern portions of the of Chicago metro \narea. Fog forecasting is always tricky, so really only medium \nconfidence in dense fog tonight, but opted to introduce some \nareas of dense fog into the grids late tonight into early Friday\nmorning. \n\nTemperatures today will once again climb into the low to mid\n70s, except in the 60s behind the lake breeze. Friday looks to \nbe a near carbon copy of today both in terms of lake breeze and \ntemps. This weekend, a strong trough is progged to track east \nacross central Canada with a trailing cold front pushing east \nacross the northern Plains toward the upper Mississippi Valley. \nThis should result in more of southerly then southwesterly \ngradient, albeit still not that strong, to develop this weekend.\nWhile not strong, it will probably be enough to hamper lake \nbreeze development and result in some slightly warmer temps \nadvecting into the area. Temperatures over the weekend could \nclimb into the mid to perhaps locally upper 70s on Sunday given \nenough sunshine. \n\nAs the strong upper ridge and omega block start breaking down,\nit will probably allow that cold front to move gradually \nsouthward across the area Monday as lays out east-west. There \nis still spread in guidance on timing of the frontal passage \nand given it will be running into the weakening upper ridge, it \nshouldn't have much push or a significant cold punch behind it. \nIt should be enough to start cooling our temps down closer to, \nbut possibly still slightly above, average by Tuesday and \nWednesday. At some point early-middle next week, shortwave \nenergy from the western trough could meander east into the \nMidwest bringing the threat for some rain to the area. \nGiven the tendency for medium range guidance to struggle with \nbreaking down blocking pattern, it isn't surprising that there \nis considerable spread in how that plays out and when/if it \nwill rain here next week. \n\n- Izzi",
